off tonight is former senior adviser to  president barack obama  valerie jarrett she's the author of the  book finding my voice  as somebody who has actually worked in a  white house valerie  what is your reaction to the trump  administration  not cooperating with a transition or  even acknowledging  uh that they lost the election well good  evening sir  first of all i could just feel so calm  listening to president-elect biden  talk about science and sensibly  encourage people to wear a mask so i was  kind of basking in the moment there for  a moment  but on a very serious note the smooth  and orderly transition of power  depends upon the president who is  stepping down  to concede in a timely fashion so that  the president who is coming in  can get up to speed and as you know i  co-chaired  president obama and vice president  biden's transition back in 2008  and even though we disagreed with  president bush about just about  everything from policies foreign and  domestic  president bush instructed his team to  cooperate with us  immediately after president obama was  elected to make sure  that we could be up to speed by the time  the inauguration came  and in return president obama directed  us eight years later  to afford president trump the same  courtesy because it's not about the  leaders it's about the country  and we know that we'll be better off  particularly during a time in the midst  of a  pandemic in the midst of a crumbling  economy  recognizing that our enemies around the  world see us as vulnerable and  weak when this happens and so for the  good of the country  one should cooperate and the practical  implication here is is that  the transition team that has been  working for a while now  um ever since uh president-elect biden  became the nominee  cannot have the resources it needs to  pay the team it cannot have  the cooperation it needs from all of the  federal agencies  until the department of general service  the general service administration  signs a memorandum of understanding with  the transition  about confidentiality and practices of  sharing that information  and so they're kind of at a limit to  what they can do  until president trump says it's okay to  cooperate  and that puts us at risk every single  day that goes by without that  cooperation  means that the learning curve becomes so  much harder and the only silver lining  of course is that vice president  the president-elect biden had been the  vice president and so he certainly  is well steeped in the white house but  there's a lot that's been happening the  last four years and having the benefit  of that  information would help  yeah that seems like a really practical  step  there needs to be a memorandum of  understanding um from the gsa that  apparently the person in charge of that  memo  is refusing to do that so that that  actually would trigger  funding um to pay the transition staff  what damage does it do to the process  and potentially the country  um in an ability for the next  administration to get to work  if they don't have these sort of i mean  bureaucratic  uh details but they matter in place  well it just means that it's harder to  hit the ground running every day that  goes by that you don't have access  to the federal agencies and the career  folks who are working there  who as a matter of practice historically  have always shared information  with the incoming administration because  again it's not about politics  this is now is really about governing  and you can govern more effectively you  can have the smooth transition  it's kind of a hallmark of our democracy  is is that when the politics are over  you behave like adults and what's  happening now  is breaking with past practice and it's  not to say  president trump he can go and explore  his alternatives in court i will say  that  according to their lawyers uh the all of  the cases are  totally frivolous and the judges have  been throwing them out right and left  but if he wants to do that he can but in  the meantime  he should cooperate with the fact that  president  uh the president-elect trump has been  designated he has more electoral college  votes there's no way that um any amount  of recounting is going to get president  trump where he wants to go  and so this is really a waste of time  and time  that when wasted hurts the country  also the republicans need to answer the  question as to how in the same  election democrats somehow rigged it to  win the white house  but not win the senate i don't  that doesn't make sense it doesn't  compute um  and you know in in speaking of those  election results obviously  joe biden was able to win uh and it's  hard to overstate the impact of black  women on that win  how much credit does do black women  organizers and voters deserve  in your view in making sure that joe  biden became the president-elect  and kamal harris became the first woman  vice president  i'm jumping in because i can't say it  fast enough enormous  credit black women have always been the  backbone of the democratic party  but this time they really got out to  work and i will tell you  there are also leaders like stacy abrams  we got to give her her props  you know she it may not have worked out  for her in georgia to become the  governor and instead of just saying okay  i didn't win and going home what did she  do  she got to work to make sure that the  election the next election  in georgia was fairer and for the first  time that i can ever remember right  georgia's  possibly blue and we have two senate  seats coming up  that are now in runoffs which could  absolutely affect the tilt of the  balance in the senate and so  all over the country black women worked  hard they showed up they campaigned  they dragged their loved ones to the  polls and i know  that president-elect uh biden and  vice president-elect harris who said it  in her speech  said black women don't always get their  due we're going to give them their due  today she said in her speech saturday  and she tweeted it today and so i know  they are appreciated by those two and  they should be appreciated by everybody  across our country  i feel like um you know every election  cycle  we we sort of speak to um the impact of  black women  in bringing democrats over the top um in  so many  races up and down the ballot in terms of  repaying that support  what can a biden harris administration  do in terms of having an explicit black  agenda  to address many of the concerns  activists in the in the streets through  the summer  and also who lobbied congress this  summer what what can they do  uh to address those concerns uh the  folks  uh in the streets were asking for  through the summer  sure so um both president-elect biden  and vice president-elect paris i think  spoke quite  eloquently over the course of the summer  as we had protests in all 50 states  people of all backgrounds races ages  saying black lives matter the calls that  president-elect biden had with the  families who'd been affected where he  showed  his empathy the way he spoke to the  nation about just  imagine what it feels like to have to  tell your children your black children  that they have to  interact differently with the police and  everyone else just try to put yourself  in those shoes i could see the pain on  his face  and the empathy when uh president vice  president-elect senator  harris was in the senate this summer she  was instrumental in trying to push  federal legislation through  it would set some minimum parameters we  know that we shouldn't have choke holes  we know that we should not  have no knock warrants we know that we  should be making sure that the people  who  swear to serve and protect actually are  doing so and that we are  have a national data bank and i'll never  forget the story  of tamira rice who was killed by a  policeman  who was fired from a neighboring  jurisdiction and then hired by the  police in cleveland well why was why  didn't that person's name show up in a  data bank why are they getting a second  chance or a third chance or fourth  chance  there are some reasonable steps that we  could take to reimagine how we police  to make sure that there is an actual  social contract between the communities  who are being served  and those who are carrying a gun and a  badge and that work is happening at the  local level  we're seeing mayors across the country  taking this issue more seriously  looking at how we're using force looking  at ways of de-escalating which is what  the police should be trying to do  establishing a better working  relationship but it would be helpful to  have a justice department  that's led by somebody that understands  that there is still systemic racism  and patterns and practice of  discriminatory behavior that should have  consequences and you would have that  you will have that under president biden  and  vice president harris for sure and so  the one  avenue that they have in addition to  legislation is a responsible independent  justice justice department  and i know that's a top priority for  them both  absolutely it's like if you have the  right set of conditions it's a lot  easier to  talk about the issues and the policies  that might address some of those  problems  if you have an administration that  denies the existence of systemic racism  it's a lot harder to get anything done  um last minute here you worked for the  first black president in the history of  the country  um and we just made history with having  a woman  a black woman and a south asian woman  elected as the first  vice president first woman vice women of  color vice president and kamala harris  um in terms of you know just how perfect  she may have to be  um in terms of not making uh mistakes  uh in similarly to the obama family in  the white house  what are your thoughts on um that line  that she may have to walk  look she spent her whole life crashing  through glass ceilings being the first  she's used to it she's used to competing  on an uneven playing field she shouldn't  have to  but she is used to it and she's up for  the job and i think what president-elect  biden recognized is having her there  as the last person who he speaks to on  every single issue both foreign and  domestic  will enable him to make better decisions  today you saw they announced the task  force on the cobia 19.  we know the only way we're going to get  our economy back on track  is if we deal with this pandemic and she  brings to the table a very intimate  knowledge of how  disproportionately this illness has  affected the black community  communities of color around our country  she understands that small businesses  that are black owned  have gone out of business at a faster  rate she understands that the essential  workers  uh the women who have lost their jobs  again disproportionately  uh women and people of color and so  bringing that set of  life experiences and perspective to the  table will enable  him to make better decisions than to  make better decisions  and so is it a higher standard because  she's the first  absolutely is she up to that absolutely  and  closing perhaps with her words that the  advice she got from her mother is just  because she's the first  doesn't mean she's going to be only the  only i know that a big  part of her agenda is to open that door  up and so  more people who look like her get to go  through that door in the future  at all levels of government  amen to that the more representation the  better it definitely matters valerie  jarrett thank you so much for being here  and stay safe  coming up

